    <section class="py-16 px-4">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose prose-lg prose-gray">

        <p class="lead text-xl text-gray-700 font-medium">
          Seventh episode of the series. The second chose the three to five indicators that matter, the fifth set up a weekly meeting that actually decides, the sixth gave a shape to what travels up to the leadership. This one looks at the tool that ties it all together: the dashboard, and what keeps it alive from one week to the next.
        </p>

        <p>
          The figure framing this episode comes from the <em>2026 Planning Intelligence Report</em> published by software vendor Board on 16 September 2026, based on 300 CFOs, CIOs and COOs at companies with annual revenues of at least $100 million. <strong>Three in four respondents say that roughly half or more of their planning decisions rely on data more than 30 days old.</strong> The survey was run by a software vendor and covers large companies: read it as a signal, not as a measure of what happens in New Caledonia. But the signal is clear: even where tools are plentiful, decisions are often made on month-old figures.
        </p>

        <h2>You already have a dashboard</h2>

        <p>
          Almost every leadership team has one, in one of three forms — and each is a sound reflex.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The shared spreadsheet</strong>: anyone can update it, it costs nothing, it adapts to everything. But nobody knows who touched it last. <strong>The charting tool</strong>: clean curves, refreshed in one click. But the figures it shows date from the last data entry, and nothing says which one. <strong>The monthly report</strong>: complete, well presented, proofread. But it arrives once the month is already over.
        </p>

        <p>
          What the three have in common: <strong>the figures are there, but nobody knows who follows them, or how old they are.</strong> A dashboard can be accurate and still have stopped.
        </p>

        <h2>Three signs of a dashboard that has stopped</h2>

        <p>
          Nothing in it is wrong. Everything is simply a little old — which is harder to spot than a mistake.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The line with no name.</strong> A figure has been falling for two weeks. Everyone assumes someone else is on it. Nobody raises the alarm, because nobody was asked to.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The six-week-old figure.</strong> It passes for today's, because no date is written next to it. It is discussed seriously in the meeting, and the decision addresses a situation that has already moved on.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>The red that lasts.</strong> The same line has been red for three months. Everyone has seen it; nothing is written next to it. After a while, the red no longer triggers anything: it has become part of the scenery.
        </p>

        <h2>A living line, four boxes</h2>

        <p>
          What follows requires no new software and no new report. It asks you to add four pieces of information to every line of the dashboard you already have.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>① A name.</strong> The person who follows this figure and speaks up when it moves. Not a department: a person. The one you call when the line changes colour.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>② A date.</strong> The day of the last update, written clearly on the line. Readers then know whether they are looking at this week or last month — and can decide accordingly.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>③ Eight weeks.</strong> The curve of the last eight weeks, rather than a single monthly figure. A single figure says where you are; a curve says where you are heading. For most topics followed by a leadership team, eight points are enough to see a trend without drowning in history.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>④ What is being done.</strong> One sentence: the action under way, or "under control, being watched". This box turns a line of information into a line of work. It is the one you review at the weekly meeting described in the fifth episode.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-navy/5 border-l-4 border-navy rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-navy mb-1">Our advice</p>
          <p class="text-navy">You already track your figures in a shared dashboard, and that is what matters most: many teams are still working from their own separate files. Start with the first two boxes only: this week, add two columns to your current dashboard, a name and a date. The curve and the action will follow naturally, as soon as every line has someone to keep it up to date.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-teal rounded-r-xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-1">Diagnostic question</p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">For each line of your dashboard, can you say within ten seconds who follows it? Do you know how old the figure you are looking at right now is? And does your oldest red line have an action written next to it?</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Why this matters in New Caledonia</h2>

        <p>
          In New Caledonia, leadership teams are lean and everyone carries several files. A dashboard rarely stays alive thanks to a dedicated department: it stays alive because one person, on each line, knows it is theirs to keep. With more activity to follow and no more people to follow it, a name and a date cost less than a new tool — and finally make the existing one useful.
        </p>

        <p>
          Your dashboard probably does not need to be rebuilt. It needs every line to have someone looking after it — a one-week job.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
          Source: Board, <em>2026 Planning Intelligence Report</em>, press release of 16 September 2026 (300 CFOs, CIOs and COOs at companies with annual revenues of at least $100 million).
        </p>

